 <iati-activity default-currency="USD" hierarchy="1" iati-activities:version="2.03" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities">
  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-PAK826</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Office will continue to assist the government in aligning its legal frameworks with the international labour Conventions, addressing any gaps, and actively considering the recommendations of the ILO supervisory bodies and Standards Review Mechanism TWG. Efforts will focus on discussing and implementing a ratification plan, prioritizing ratification of identified ILS and supporting gap analyses where needed. The government will be supported in organizing social partners and provincial counterparts to raise awareness on the importance of ratifying up-to-date Conventions, positioning Pakistan as a responsible state and ethical business partner. For ratified Conventions, the office will continue supporting the ILS reporting committees and the federal government to start using new ILO reporting system from 2027, and in ensuring quality reports and responses to comments from the ILO&apos;s supervisory mechanism. Additionally, the ILO will continue supporting legal reforms at the provincial level and help facilitate consultations among stakeholders and social partners in these areas.

Building on prior work in sectors like textiles, the office will implement a code of practice on occupational safety and health (OSH) and chemicals in textiles, clothing, leather, and footwear in selected textile mills in Karachi.

In addition to the textiles sector, the office will implement other sectoral codes and tools in shipbreaking, sanitation, construction, and health, aiming to develop sector-specific initiatives throughout the country.</narrative>
  </description>
